@startuml client_send_queue_drain_activity
title ClientSendQueue - queued send dispatch

start
:DrainUnderLock(lock);
note right
    Entry: the caller holds lifetime_.mutex_ through lock.
    The procedure releases it around transport and callbacks,
    then reacquires it before changing queue or slot state.
end note

:Create ClientConnectionLifetime::Operation under the caller's lock;
if (Operation rejected admission?) then (yes)
    :Return with lock held: no dispatch after stopping;
    stop
else (no)
    :Retain the admitted operation for the entire drain continuation;
endif
while (queue_ is non-empty and lifetime_.IsAccepting()?) is (yes)
    :Take the front command and remove it from queue_;
    :Create a live local ReplySlotToken for this dispatch;
    :Move command.callback into a local ReplyCallback;
    if (local callback is non-empty?) then (yes)
        :Move the callback into waiting_for_reply_ and record this sender;
        :SendTransport(operation, REQUEST, command.message);
        :Return the command to the front of pool_;
        if (Transport succeeded?) then (yes)
            :Clear this sender owner if it still owns the pending callback;
            :End operation scope (RAII);
            :Return with lock held and transferred completion preserved;
            stop
        else (no)
            :Check whether this sender still owns the slot;
            if (This sender owns the slot?) then (yes)
                :Move the reply callback locally, keeping the optional engaged;
                :Unlock lifetime_.mutex_;
                if (local callback is non-empty?) then (yes)
                    :Invoke the callback with the transport error;
                else (no)
                    :Skip an empty callback;
                endif
                :Relock lifetime_.mutex_;
                if (This sender still owns the slot?) then (yes)
                    :Reset waiting_for_reply_ and its sender owner;
                else (no)
                    :Preserve another path's slot;
                endif
            else (no)
                :Input or stopping already took completion;
                :Continue to the next queue/admission check with lock held;
            endif
        endif
    else (no)
        :Reserve an empty ReplyCallback marker owned by this sender;
        :SendTransport(operation, SEND, command.message);
        :Ignore the transport result;
        :Return the command to the front of pool_;
        if (This sender still owns the marker?) then (yes)
            :Reset waiting_for_reply_ and its sender owner;
        else (no)
            :Preserve another path's slot;
        endif
    endif
endwhile (no)

:End operation scope (RAII);
:Return with lock held;
stop

legend
    The queue is FIFO; recycled free commands are reused LIFO.
    A detached command stays reserved until transport finishes.
    The active operation covers transport, recycling, and error callback return.
    Every transport helper releases lifetime_.mutex_ for IPC and relocks afterward.
    The request callback moves into waiting_for_reply_ before transport.
    Sender identity uses a live local token, independent of callback moves.
    After transport returns, the SendCommand is recycled into pool_.
    Its message storage can be reused independently of the pending callback.
    A still-pending callback keeps the reply slot engaged and pauses queue dispatch.
    Input or stopping may already have taken completion during unlocked transport.
    Failed requests invoke their callback only if completion was not already taken.
    The engaged slot also keeps reentrant requests queued during that callback.
    An empty callback selects SEND, not REQUEST, in this procedure.
    An empty queue at entry leaves the incoming reply-slot state unchanged.
    Stopping prevents the next queued send; the last operation can schedule finalization.
    This is local dispatch control flow, not full callback-composition proof.
endlegend

@enduml